                  %a{:href => "#van_kampen_theorem_in_homotopy_type_theory"} van Kampen theorem in homotopy type theory
              %li
                %a{:href => "#generalizations"} Generalizations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em Seifert-van Kampen theorem
            is a classical theorem in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            which computes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            of a pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            in terms of a decomposition into
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
          %p
            It is most naturally expressed by saying that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            preserves certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimits"} homotopy colimits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
            (i.e. a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-colimit"} 2-colimit
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-colimit"} strict colimit
            %em homotopy van Kampen theorems
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em strict van Kampen theorems
          %h2#statement Statement
          %h3#for_fundamental_groups For fundamental groups
          %p
            The most classical version of the
            %strong Seifert-van Kampen theorem
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+group"} fundamental group
            is the following.
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covered
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U,V \subset X
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \cap V
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path-connected+space"} path connected
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∩
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U \cap V
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
              of
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi U
                            %mo ∩
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi U
                            %mo ,
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi V
                            %mo ,
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\pi_1(U \cap V, x) &amp;\to&amp; \pi_1(U,x)
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    \\pi_1(V,x) &amp;\to&amp; \pi_1(X,x)
                    }
            %p
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
              square in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
          %h3#from_groups_to_groupoids From groups to groupoids
          %p
            We can regard the classical theorem as being a statement about fundamental groupoids as follows. Recall that any group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            can be considered as a 1-object groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}
            takes colimits of groups to strict colimits of groupoids. Moreover, a strict pushout of one-object groupoids is also a 2-pushout, because the maps we are pushing out are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bo+functor"} bijective on objects
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrations"} cofibrations
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical model structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gpd
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo ∩
                          %mi V
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi V
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %msub
                            %mi π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\mathbf{B}\pi_1(U \cap V, x) &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}\pi_1(U,x)
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\mathbf{B}\pi_1(V,x) &amp;\to&amp; \mathbf{B}\pi_1(X,x)
                  }
          %p
            which is both a strict pushout and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-colimit"} 2-pushout
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gpd
          %p
            Now let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X'
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+component"} path component
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            which contains
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\cap V
            (there is exactly one such, because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\cap V
            is path-connected), and similarly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U'
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V'
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X')
            is equivalent to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\pi_1(X,x)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo ∩
                          %mi V
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi U
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi V
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  \\Pi_1(U \cap V) &amp;\to&amp; \Pi_1(U')
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \\Pi_1(V') &amp;\to&amp; \Pi_1(X')
                  }
          %p
            This is an instance of what we referred to above as a
            = succeed "." do
              %em homotopy van Kampen theorem
            %em strict van Kampen theorem
            as well.
          %p
            Note that the functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(U\cap V) \to \Pi_1(U')
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(U\cap V) \to \Pi_1(U')
            are injective on objects, hence cofibrations in the canonical model structure, and so their strict pushout is also a 2-pushout. This strict pushout is therefore equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X')
            %em fundamental groupoid on a set of specified basepoints
            as below.)
          %p
            We now consider more general versions of the van Kampen theorem which do not require
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\cap V
            to be path-connected, and which compute all of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            rather than just
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X')
          %h3#strict_van_kampen_theorem_for_groupoids Strict van Kampen theorem for groupoids
          %p
            Let us define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X,X_0)
            of a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and a subset
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \subseteq X
            to be the full subgroupoid of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
            on the set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is given as a subspace of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 \subseteq X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(U,X_0)
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(U,X_0\cap U)
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo *
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_* =(X,X_0)
            is a pair consisting of a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            of base points, we say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_*
            is
            %em connected
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            meets each path component of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X,X_0)
            to be equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X)
          %p
            Now let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U^i \to X\}
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in I
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+cover"} open cover
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d=(i,j) \in I^2
            we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^d
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∩
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^i \cap U^j
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-tuple" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e\in I^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi U
                    %mo *
                    %mi e
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^e_*
            be the pair
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi e
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∩
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi e
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U^e,X_0 \cap U^e)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequaliser"} coequaliser
            diagram of pairs of spaces, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b,c
            are determined by inclusions:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨆
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi U
                    %mo *
                    %mi d
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mi b
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi a
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨆
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                  %msubsup
                    %mi U
                    %mo *
                    %mi i
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo *
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\bigsqcup_{d \in I^2} U^d_*
                  \\stackrel{\overset{a}{\to}}{\underset{b}{\to}}
                  \\bigsqcup _{i \in I} U^i_* \xrightarrow{c} X_*
                  \\,.
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              If the pairs of spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi U
                      %mo *
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^f_*
              are connected for all 1-, 2-, or 3-fold intersections
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi U
                      %mo *
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^f_*
              of the pairs
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi U
                        %mo *
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^i_*
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  (Conn) The pair
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_*
                  is connected; and
              %li
                %p
                  (Iso) The
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
                  functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1
                  takes the above coequaliser diagram of pairs of spaces to a (strict) coequaliser diagram of groupoids.
          %p
            The limit “3” on the connectivity hypothesis may be analyzed as “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+2
            ”, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            is the categorical dimension of the fundamental groupoids we are considering and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            is a universal constant. This becomes evident when generalizing to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy+van+Kampen+theorems"} higher homotopy van Kampen theorems
          %p
            In one proof of the theorem (due to Brown and Razak, see below), the number 3 arises from the Lebesgue covering dimension of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            %em all
            subpaths join points of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+square"} commutative square
          %p
            In another, more categorical proof explained in the next section, the number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3
            arises from the fact that 2-colimits can be computed using coproducts and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/descent+objects"} descent objects
            of 2-truncated
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+objects"} simplicial objects
          %p
            Note that the theorem about groupoids does
            %em not
            reduce to a theorem about groups. For example,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may be a connected space which is the union of two open sets each with many components and whose intersection has many path components. Such examples arise commonly from applications to combinatorial group theory; consider for example a covering space of a wedge of spaces. Or the connected
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may be the union of 23 open sets whose three fold intersections have 123 path components. In each case the fundamental group one might want to calculate is in the middle of this complicated combinatorial situation, but at least the theorem has turned a topological problem into a group theory and combinatorial problem, and the remarkable fact is that the fundamental groups are
            %em completely
            determined by the theorem. This is an “anomaly” for traditional algebraic topology, where invariants relating adjacent dimensions may be determined by exact sequences which do not give complete information. The reason for the success seems to be that groupoids have structure in dimensions 0 and 1, and so can model the gluing of spaces.
          %p
            This suggests that
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy+van+Kampen+theorem"} higher homotopy van Kampen theorem
          %h3#homotopy_van_kampen_theorem_for_groupoids Homotopy van Kampen theorem for groupoids
          %p From a 2-categorical point of view, the strict van Kampen theorem above can be decomposed into the following parts:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                When
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is the union of a family of open sets, then it is the homotopy colimit of a diagram involving these open sets.
            %li
              %p The fundamental groupoid functor preserves homotopy colimits (i.e. takes them to homotopy colimits, or equivalently 2-colimits, of groupoids).
            %li
              %p Certain 2-colimits of groupoids can be computed over “2-truncated” subdiagrams.
            %li
              %p In certain situations, strict colimits of groupoids are also 2-colimits.
          %p
            We can then prove the theorem by proving each of these three parts separately. This is essentially accomplished in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Farjoun"} Farjoun
          #Cechcover.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              If a space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the union of a family of open sets
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U^i)_{i\in I}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hocolim
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo ∼
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hocolim C_\bullet(U) \xrightarrow{\sim} X,
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_\bullet(U)
              is the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cover"} Cech cover
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+space"} simplicial space
              defined by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi J
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi I
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi J
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mo =
                        %mi n
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi J
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_n(U) = \coprod_{J\subseteq I,\, |J|=n} \bigcap_{i\in J} U^i.
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#DuggerIsaksen01"} Dugger-Isaksen 01
          #Pi1Hocolim.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_4 Theorem
            %p
              Suppose
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              is a small category and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X^i)_{i\in I}
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagram"} diagram
              of spaces. Then we have an equivalence of groupoids
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} hocolim
                      %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} hocolim
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(\hocolim_i X^i) \simeq \hocolim_i \Pi_1(X^i).
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1
              is, up to equivalence, the composite of itself with the 1st
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+approximation"} Postnikov approximation
              functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi P
                        %mn 1
                      %mo :
                      %mi Top
                      %mo →
                      %mi Top
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1 : Top \to Top
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_1
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bousfield+localization"} Bousfield localization
              functor for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
              (for localizing at the map
              = succeed ")," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                      %mo →
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2 \to \ast
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1
              is part of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
              between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gpd
              and the 1-localized model structure on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Top
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top
          #Codescent.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_5 Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is the union of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U^i)_{i\in I}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0\subseteq X
              is a set of basepoints which intersects each 1-, 2-, and 3-fold intersection of opens
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^i
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X,X_0)
              is equivalent to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-colimit"} 2-colimit
              (i.e.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codescent+object"} codescent object
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                        %mo ,
                        %mi k
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                        %mi k
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munderover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\coprod_{i,j,k} \Pi_1(U^{i j k},X_0) \;\underoverset{\to}{\to}{\to}\;
                    \\coprod_{i,j} \Pi_1(U^{i j},X_0) \;\rightrightarrows\;
                    \\coprod_i \Pi_1(U^i,X_0).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              If we neglect the
              = succeed "\u2018s," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Pi1Hocolim"}
              to Theorem
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Cechcover"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gpd
              are equivalent to codescent objects of their 2-truncations (essentially because groupoids are 1-groupoids), this has an equivalent homotopy colimit. Finally, the assumption on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0
              ensures that restricting to it as a set of basepoints does not change the equivalence type of any of the groupoids in the 2-truncated diagram.
          #CodescentCoeq.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_6 Theorem
            %p
              Under the hypotheses of Theorem
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Codescent"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X,X_0)
              is isomorphic to the strict coequalizer of
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ,
                        %mi j
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∐
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\coprod_{i,j} \Pi_1(U^{i j},X_0) \;\rightrightarrows\;
                    \\coprod_i \Pi_1(U^i,X_0).
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo =
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = P(I)
              be the power set of the indexing set
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi Gpd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:A\to Gpd
              defined by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi J
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi Π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋂
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi J
                      %msup
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(J) = \Pi_1(\bigcap_{i\in J} U^i, X_0)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is equivalent to the codescent object in Theorem
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Codescent"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mrow
                        %mo ≤
                        %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_{\le 3}(I) \to A
              is “2-final”.)
            %p
              However,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is cofibrant in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+model+structure"} projective model structure
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Gpd
                        %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gpd^A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                    %mi Gpd
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi ob
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\xrightarrow{F} Gpd \xrightarrow{ob} Set
              is free (relative to the forgetful functor
              = succeed "):" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi Set
                        %mrow
                          %mi ob
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^A \to Set^{ob(A)}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi J
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in F(J)
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi J
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J = \{ i\in I \,|\, x\in U^i \}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is equivalent to its strict colimit, which in turn is easily seen to be the coequalizer shown above.
            %p
              This shows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(X,X_0)
              is equivalent to the strict coequalizer shown above. But this equivalence is clearly also bijective on objects, so it is an isomorphism of groupoids.
          %h3#examples Examples
          %p
            Let
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X=S^1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∖
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U= S^1 \setminus \{N\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∖
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V= S^1 \setminus \{S\}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            are antipodal points. Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi V
                  %mo ≅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\cong V\cong (0,1)
            are contractible, while
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi V
                    %mo ≅
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\cap V \cong (0,1) \sqcup (0,1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                          %mo ⊔
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{ \ast\sqcup \ast &amp; \to &amp; \ast \\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow \\
                  \\ast &amp; \to &amp; \Pi_1(S^1) }
          %p
            It’s not hard to compute that this 2-pushout must be
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}\mathbb{Z}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_0 = \{u,v\}
            of two basepoints with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\in U
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v\in V
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo *
                          %mo ⊔
                          %mo *
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℐ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi ℐ
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Π
                            %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi S
                            %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi X
                            %mn 0
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{ \ast\sqcup \ast &amp; \to &amp; \mathcal{I} \\
                  \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow \\
                  \\mathcal{I} &amp; \to &amp; \Pi_1(S^1,X_0) }
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℐ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{I}
            is the invertible
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(U,X_0)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi X
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1(V,X_0)
          %p
            Note that the version of the theorem for fundamental groups does not apply, since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U\cap V
            is not path-connected.
          %h3#van_kampen_theorem_in_homotopy_type_theory van Kampen theorem in homotopy type theory
          %p
            The van Kampen theorem can be expressed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+homotopy+theory"} synthetic homotopy theory
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B, C
            be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \to B
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : A \to C
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ≡
                  %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊔
                    %mi A
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P\equiv B \sqcup_A C
            be their
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout"} pushout
          %p
            By defining a special
            %em code
            function using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/encode-decode+method"} encode-decode method
            the van Kampen theorem says that:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Π
                    %mn 1
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi code
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi u
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi_1 P(u, v) \simeq code(u,v)
          %p
            There are (at least) two code functions that can be given, which are listed in section 8.7 of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+Book"} HoTT Book
          %h2#generalizations Generalizations
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+theorem+for+toposes"} van Kampen theorem for toposes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy+van+Kampen+theorem"} higher homotopy van Kampen theorem
          %h2#References References
          %p The Seifert-van Kampen theorem is named after
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Herbert+Seifert"}> Herbert Seifert
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Konstruction drei dimensionaler geschlossener Räume
                %em 83
                (1931) 26–66
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Egbert+van+Kampen"}> Egbert van Kampen
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On the connection between the fundamental groups of some related spaces
                %strong 55
                (1933) 261–267 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/51000091"}> jstor:51000091
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Introduction and review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronald+Brown"}> Ronald Brown
                , §6.7 &amp; §8.4 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Elements of Modern Topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://archive.org/details/elementsofmodern0000rona/page/n1/mode/2up"} ark:/13960/s26wmm5260h
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Brown-Topology.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"}> Peter May
                , chapter 2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/A+Concise+Course+in+Algebraic+Topology"} A Concise Course in Algebraic Topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.press.uchicago.edu/ucp/books/book/chicago/C/bo3777031.html"} ISBN: 9780226511832
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~may/CONCISE/ConciseRevised.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Hatcher02
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Allen+Hatcher"}> Allen Hatcher
                , section 1.2 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic Topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/gb/academic/subjects/mathematics/geometry-and-topology/algebraic-topology-1?format=PB=9780521795401"} ISBN:9780521795401
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATpage.html"} webpage
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia:
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seifert%E2%80%93Van_Kampen_theorem#References"} Seifert-van Kampen theorem
          %p The use of a set of base points for a pushout theorem and so determining the fundamental group of the circle was published in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Groupoids and van Kampen’s theorem
          %p The more general theorem for the fundamental groupoid on a set of base points:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"} Ronnie Brown
              and A. Razak,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A van Kampen theorem for unions of non-connected spaces
              %a{:href => "http://pages.bangor.ac.uk/pdffiles/brown-razak.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            See also this
            %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/40945/compelling-evidence-that-two-basepoints-are-better-than-one"} mathoverflow discussion
          %p
            The pushout theorem is among applications of groupoids to
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://groupoids.org.uk/topgpds.html"} Topology and Groupoids
          %p The proof for pushouts is discussed in some detail in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Higgins"} Philip Higgins
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rafael+Sivera"} Rafael Sivera
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nonabelian+Algebraic+Topology"} Nonabelian Algebraic Topology
          %p as a background to the following chapters on versions for crossed modules and crossed complexes, i.e. for higher dimensional versions.
          %p The proof via homotopy colimits is roughly taken from
          %ul
            %li#Farjoun
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emmanuel+Dror+Farjoun"}> Emmanuel Dror Farjoun
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fundamental group of homotopy colimits
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/papers/farjoun.pdf"} pdf
          %p Other references cited above:
          %ul
            %li#DuggerIsaksen01
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Dugger"}> Dan Dugger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Isaksen"} Daniel Isaksen
              %em Hypercovers in topology
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0111287"}> arxiv:math/0111287
              )
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homotopy"} persistent homotopy
            with focus on the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+theorem"} van Kampen theorem
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excision"} excision
            and the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurewicz+theorem"} Hurewicz theorem
          %ul
            %li
              Mehmet Ali Batan, Mehmetcik Pamuk, Hanife Varli,
              %em Persistent Homotopy
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.08865"} arXiv:1909.08865
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p
            For the van Kampen theorem in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li
              Univalent Foundations Project,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
              (2013)
          %p
            Formalization of the van Kampen theorem in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistants"} proof assistants
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/HoTT/HoTT-Agda/tree/e48a16bcd719e2fcb409d79e3f7df6c6b81223bb/theorems/homotopy/vankampen"} In Agda
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/leanprover/lean2/blob/master/hott/homotopy/vankampen.hlean"} In Lean
        .revisedby
